art.*  \n   Use the \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\)\n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y=x-(i-1)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k). \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   For a point \\((a,b)\\) we have \\(b-a=-(i-1)\\) exactly when it lies on \\(L_i\\).  \n   Consequently the family \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose difference  \n\n   \\[\n   d:=b-a\\in\\{-(k-1),-(k-2),\\dots ,0\\}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *Non‑sunny part.*  \n   Introduce only **vertical** and **horizontal** lines:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   V_j &: x=j \\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,u),\\\\\n   H_j &: y=j \\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,v).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The total number of lines in (5) is \\(u+v=m=n-k\\), exactly the required number of non‑sunny lines.\n\n   *Verification that every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered.*\n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) and set \\(d=b-a\\).\n\n   1. **If \\(d\\in\\{-(k-1),\\dots ,0\\}\\)** then (4) shows that \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_{\\,d+k}\\) from (3).\n\n   2. **If \\(d\\ge 1\\).**  \n      From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(d\\ge1\\) we obtain  \n\n      \\[\n      2a = (a+b)-(b-a) \\le (n+1)-1 = n .\n      \\]\n\n      Hence \\(a\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  \n      Because \\(m=n-k\\ge\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) for every admissible \\(k\\) (this is exactly the content of (2)), we have \\(a\\le u\\) or \\(a\\ge u+1\\).\n\n      *If \\(a\\le u\\)* then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n      *If \\(a>u\\)* then \\(a\\ge u+1\\). Since \\(a\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\le v\\) (again by (2)), we have \\(b\\le v\\) and consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).\n\n   3. **If \\(d\\le -k\\).**  \n      This case is symmetric to the previous one after swapping the roles of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\).  \n      Indeed, set \\(d' = a-b = -d\\ge k\\). From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we get \\(2b\\le n\\), so \\(b\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\le v\\).  \n\n      *If \\(b\\le v\\)* then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\