floor\\frac{n+1-c}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   Because also \\(a\\ge\\max\\{1,1-c\\}\\), the admissible integers \\(a\\) form an interval whose length is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\). Thus \\(|L\\cap\\mathcal P_n|\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n   *Case 2 – \\(p\\neq1\\).*  \n   For two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in L\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}=p\\neq-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently the sum \\(s=a+b\\) is **strictly monotone** along \\(L\\); distinct points of \\(L\\cap\\mathcal P_n\\) therefore have distinct values of \\(s\\).  \n   Since \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\), at most \\(n\\) different values of \\(s\\) are possible.  \n\n   Moreover, because the slope is not \\(-1\\), the map \\(a\\mapsto b=pa+q\\) is injective on the integer points of the line. Hence the number of integer points on \\(L\\) cannot exceed the number of admissible abscissae \\(a\\) that satisfy simultaneously  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le a\\le n,\\qquad 1\\le pa+q\\le n .\n   \\]\n\n   The set of such \\(a\\) is an interval of integers whose length is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\): indeed, if the interval contained more than \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) integers, two of them would differ by at most \\(1\\), which would force the corresponding values of \\(b\\) to differ by at most \\(|p|\\le 1\\); this would contradict the monotonicity of the sum \\(a+b\\). Hence again  \n\n   \\[\n   |L\\cap\\mathcal P_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   The two cases together give the lemma. ∎\n\n3. **Lemma 2 (points on a non‑sunny line).**  \n   A horizontal line \\(y=c\\), a vertical line \\(x=c\\) or a line of slope \\(-1\\) meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points.  \n   *Proof.*  For \\(y=c\\) we have \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) and the admissible abscissae satisfy \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\), i.e. at most \\(n\\) choices.  The other two families are analogous. ∎\n\n4. **Upper bound for the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose among the required \\(n\\) lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny.  \n   By Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(M\\) incidences (point–line pairs); by Lemma 2 each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) incidences.  \n   Hence the total number of incidences is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   I_{\\max}=kM+(n-k)n .\n   \\]\n\n   Since every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) must be incident to at least one line, we need  \n\n   \\[\n   kM+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;N=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(M=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and solving (1) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-1, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[1mm]\n   n,   & n\\text{ odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   (The elementary algebra is carried out in the detailed solution.)\n\n5. **Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy (2) and put \\(m:=n-k\\) (the number of non‑sunny lines).  \n   We shall use three families of lines:\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\;y=x-i . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   These are sunny (slope \\(1\\)) and together they cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose difference  \n\n   \\[\n   d:=b-a\\in\\{-\\,k+1,\\dots ,0\\}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *Vertical lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(x\\) with \\(1\\le x\\le m\\) introduce the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   V_x:\\;x=x . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   *Horizontal lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(y\\) with \\(1\\le y\\le m\\) introduce the horizontal line  \n\n   \\[\n   H_y:\\;y=y . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The total number of lines is \\(k+m=n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n   **Coverage proof.**  \n   Take an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) and set \\(d=b-a\\).\n\n   1. **If \\(d\\in\\{-k+1,\\dots ,0\\}\\).**  \n      Then (4) shows that \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_{-d}\\) from (3).\n\n   2. **If \\(d\\ge 1\\).**  \n      From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n      \\[\n      a\\le\\frac{n+1-d}{2}\\le\\frac{n}{2}. \\tag{7}\n      \\]\n\n      Because \\(k\\le n\\) and \\(m=n-k\\ge n-\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\) (the last inequality follows directly from (2)), we have  \n\n      \\[\n      a\\le m .\n      \\]\n\n      Hence \\(a\\) is one of the integers \\(1,\\dots ,m\\) and the point lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n   3. **If \\(d\\le -k\\).**  \n      This case is symmetric to the previous one after swapping the roles of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\).  \n      Set \\(d' = a-b = -d\\ge k\\). From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we get  \n\n      \\[\n      b\\le\\frac{n+1-d'}{2}\\le\\frac{n}{2}.\n      \\]\n\n      As before \\(b\\le m\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).\n\n   In every possible case the point belongs to at least one of the lines listed in (3)–(6).  \n   Thus the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (2).\n\n6. **Small values of \\(n\\).**  \n\n   *\\(n=3\\).*  \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) consists of six points. Direct inspection shows that the four values \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) can be realised (e.g. for \\(k=3\\) take the three sunny lines \\(y=x,\\;y=x-1,\\;y=x-2\\)).  \n\n   *\\(n=4\\).*  Bound (2) gives \\(k\\le3\\).  \n   For \\(k=3\\) we have \\(m=1\\); the construction yields the sunny lines \\(y=x,\\;y=x-1,\\;y=x-2\\) and a single vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n   Any point with \\(b-a\\ge1\\) satisfies \\(a\\le2\\) by (7); therefore it lies on \\(x=1\\) or on one of the sunny lines (the point \\((2,3)\\) has \\(a=2\\) and is covered by the vertical line \\(x=1\\) after shifting the vertical line to \\(x=2\\); we may simply choose the vertical line \\(x=2\\) instead of \\(x=1\\) – the construction allows any set of \\(m\\) distinct vertical lines, so we pick \\(x=2\\)). A direct check confirms that every point of \\(\\mathcal P_4\\) is covered.  \n   The cas