            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous solution.  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ge 4\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\},   & n\\ge 5\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, when \\(n\\) is even at most \\(n-1\\) of the \\(n\\) lines can be sunny, while when \\(n\\) is odd all \\(n\\) lines may be sunny.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad\n   N:=|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lemma 1 (maximal number of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on a sunny line).**  \n   If \\(L\\) is a line whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (i.e. a *sunny* line) then  \n\n   \\[\n   |L\\cap\\mathcal P_n|\\le M:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{L1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Write \\(L\\) as \\(y=px+q\\) with \\(p\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \n   If two distinct integer points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in L\\) are given, then  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}=p .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the slope \\(p\\) is rational; write it in lowest terms \\(p=\\dfrac{u}{v}\\) with\n   \\(u\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;v\\in\\mathbb N,\\;\\gcd(u,v)=1\\) and \\(v\\ge 1\\).  \n   From the equality above we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   a_2-a_1=v\\,t,\\qquad b_2-b_1=u\\,t\\qquad\\text{for some }t\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently the change of the sum \\(s=a+b\\) between the two points is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Delta s=(a_2-a_1)+(b_2-b_1)=t\\,(v+u).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(p\\neq-1\\) we have \\(v+u\\neq0\\); moreover \\(v\\ge1\\) and \\(|u|\\le v\\) would give\n   \\(|p|\\le1\\), which is impossible for a sunny line (the only slopes with \\(|p|\\le1\\) are\n   \\(0,\\pm1,\\infty\\)). Hence \\(|v+u|\\ge 2\\).  \n   From (2) we see that the sum \\(s=a+b\\) changes by at least \\(2\\) when we move from one\n   integer point of \\(L\\) to another.  \n\n   All points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfy \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\); therefore at most\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) different values of \\(s\\) can occur on a sunny\n   line, and each value of \\(s\\) corresponds to at most one point of \\(L\\cap\\mathcal P_n\\).\n   Hence \\(|L\\cap\\mathcal P_n|\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\), proving (L1). ∎\n\n3. **Lemma 2 (points on a non‑sunny line).**  \n   A horizontal line \\(y=c\\), a vertical line \\(x=c\\) or a line of slope \\(-1\\) meets\n   \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points.  \n   *Proof.*  For \\(y=c\\) we have \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) and the admissible abscissae satisfy\n   \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\), i.e. at most \\(n\\) choices.  The other two families are analogous. ∎\n\n4. **Upper bound for the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose among the required \\(n\\) lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny.  \n   By Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(M\\) incidences (point–line pairs);\n   by Lemma 2 each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) incidences.  \n   Hence the total number of incidences is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   I_{\\max}=kM+(n-k)n .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Since every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) must be incident to at least one line,\n   we need  \n\n   \\[\n   kM+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;N=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(M=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and solving (4) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-1, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[1mm]\n   n,   & n\\text{ odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   (The elementary algebra is carried out in the detailed solution.)\n\n5. **Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy (5) and put  \n\n   \\[\n   m:=n-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of non‑sunny lines}).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   We shall use three families of lines:\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\;y=x-i .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   These are sunny (slope \\(1\\)) and together they cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose\n   difference  \n\n   \\[\n   d:=b-a\\in\\{-\\,k+1,\\dots ,0\\}.\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines.*  \n   We split the \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines into three sub‑families, according to the size of\n   \\(m\\).\n\n   *Case A – \\(m\\ge 2\\).*  \n   Choose  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor),\\qquad\n   H_\\ell:\\;y=\\ell\\qquad(\\ell=1,\\dots ,\\lceil m/2\\rceil).\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus we have exactly \\(\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor+\\lceil m/2\\rceil=m\\) non‑sunny lines,\n   all vertical or horizontal.\n\n   *Case B – \\(m=1\\).*  \n   Use the single line  \n\n   \\[\n   D:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   This line is not sunny (its slope is \\(-1\\)) and contains every point of\n   \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\).\n\n   **Coverage proof.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) and set \\(d=b-a\\).\n\n   1. **If \\(d\\in\\{-k+1,\\dots ,0\\}\\).**  \n      Then (8) shows that \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_{-d}\\) from (7).\n\n   2. **If \\(d\\ge 1\\).**  \n      From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n      \\[\n      a\\le\\frac{n+1-d}{2}\\le\\frac{n}{2}.\n      \\tag{11}\n      \\]\n\n      *When \\(m\\ge 2\\).*  \n      By (5) we have \\(k\\le n-1\\) for even \\(n\\) and \\(k\\le n\\) for odd \\(n\\); in both\n      situations  \n\n      \\[\n      \\frac{n}{2}\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n      \\frac{n}{2}\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{m}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n      \\]\n\n      Consequently \\(a\\) is one of the integers \\(1,\\dots ,\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor\\) or\n      \\(1,\\dots ,\\lceil m/2\\rceil\\).  \n      If \\(a\\le\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor\\) the point lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\); otherwise\n      \\(a\\le\\lceil m/2\\rceil\\) and the point lies on the horizontal line \\(H_a\\) (note that\n      a horizontal line \\(y=\\ell\\) also contains the point because \\(b=a+d\\le a+\\frac{n}{2}\\le\n      n\\)). In any case the point is covered by one of the lines in (9).\n\n      *When \\(m=1\\).*  \n      Then \\(k=n-1\\) (the only possible value when \\(n\\) is even) or \\(k=n\\) (when \\(n\\) is\n      odd). In both sub‑cases we have \\(d\\ge 1\\) implies \\(a\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\le n-1\\); the\n      unique non‑sunny line is \\(D:x+y=n+1\\). Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and equality holds\n      precisely when \\(d\\ge 1\\) and \\(a\\) attains its maximal possible value, the point\n      \\((a,b)\\) either lies on a sunny line (if \\(d\\le k-1\\)) or, when \\(a+b=n+1\\), lies on\n      \\(D\\). Hence it is covered.\n\n   3. **If \\(d\\le -k\\).**  \n      This case is symmetric to the previous one after swapping the roles of \\(a\\) and\n      \\(b\\). Indeed, set \\(d' = a-b = -d\\ge k\\). From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n      \\[\n      b\\le\\frac{n+1-d'}{2}\\le\\frac{n}{2}.\n      \\]\n\n      The same argument as in 2, with the vertical/horizontal families interchanged,\n      shows that \\((a,b)\\) lies on one of the lines listed in (9) when \\(m\\ge2\\), and on\n      the line \\(D\\) when \\(m=1\\).\n\n   In every possible case the