2-a_1)+(b_2-b_1)=t\\,(v+u).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(p\\neq-1\\) we have \\(v+u\\neq0\\); moreover \\(|v+u|\\ge 1\\).  \n   Starting from any point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) the value of \\(s\\) can increase or\n   decrease by at most \\(|v+u|\\) when we move to another integer point of the same\n   line.  Since all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfy \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\), at most\n   \\(n\\) different values of \\(s\\) can appear on a single line, and each value of\n   \\(s\\) determines at most one point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (the intersection of the\n   line \\(a+b=s\\) with \\(L\\) is unique).  Hence \\(|L\\cap\\mathcal P_n|\\le n\\). ∎\n\n3. **Lemma 2 (maximum number of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on a non‑sunny line).**  \n   A horizontal line \\(y=c\\), a vertical line \\(x=c\\) or a line of slope \\(-1\\) meets\n   \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points.  The proof is immediate from the\n   definition of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n4. **Upper bound for the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose among the required \\(n\\) lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny.  \n   By Lemma 1 each sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); by\n   Lemma 2 each non‑sunny line also contains at most \\(n\\) points.  Therefore the\n   total number of incidences (point–line pairs) is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   I_{\\max}=k\\cdot n+(n-k)\\cdot n=n^{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) must be incident to at least one line,\n   we need  \n\n   \\[\n   n^{2}\\ge N=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 ,\n   \\]\n\n   which is true for every \\(n\\ge 1\\).  Consequently **no restriction** on \\(k\\)\n   follows from incidence counting; any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is\n   *a priori* possible.\n\n5. **Construction that realises every \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\).**  \n   Fix \\(k\\) and put \\(m:=n-k\\) (the number of non‑sunny lines).  We shall use three\n   families of lines.\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\