lope \\(-1\\) meets\n   \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points.  This follows directly from the\n   definition of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n4. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   If among the required \\(n\\) lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny, then by Lemma\u202f1 each\n   sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and by Lemma\u202f2 each\n   non\u2011sunny line also contains at most \\(n\\) points.  Therefore the total number\n   of incidences (point\u2013line pairs) is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   I_{\\max}=k\\cdot n+(n-k)\\cdot n=n^{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) must be incident to at least one line,\n   we need \\(n^{2}\\ge N=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\), which holds for all \\(n\\ge 1\\).  Hence\n   **no restriction** on \\(k\\) follows from counting; any integer\n   \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is *a priori* possible.\n\n5. **Construction that realises every \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\).**  \n   Fix \\(k\\) and put  \n\n   \\[\n   m:=n-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of non\u2011sunny lines}).\n   \\]\n\n   We shall use three families of lines.\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For \\(c=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_c:\\; y = x + c .\n   \\tag{5.1}\n   \\]\n\n   These lines have slope \\(1\\) (hence are sunny) and together they cover all\n   points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose difference  \n\n   \\[\n   d:=b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}.\n   \\tag{5.2}\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed, \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b-a=c\\) exactly when it lies on \\(S_c\\).\n\n   *Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n\n   - If \\(m=0\\) (i.e. \\(k=n\\)) we need no non\u2011sunny line; the \\(n\\) sunny lines\n     \\(S_0,\\dots ,S_{n-1}\\) already cover \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) because for any\n     \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(0\\le b-a\\le n-1\\).\n\n   - If \\(m\\ge 1\\) we take the **vertical** lines  \n\n     \\[\n     V_j:\\; x=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,m)\n     \\tag{5.3}\n     \\]\n\n     (all non\u2011sunny).  No horizontal lines 