tarrow\\quad\n   v(b_2-b_1)=u(a_2-a_1).                                   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   Adding the two equalities \\(a_i+b_i=s_i\\) (\\(i=1,2\\)) gives\n   \\[\n   s_2-s_1=(a_2-a_1)+(b_2-b_1)= (a_2-a_1)+\\frac{u}{v}(a_2-a_1)\n   =\\frac{u+v}{v}(a_2-a_1).                                 \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Because \\(\\gcd(u,v)=1\\) and \\(u+v\\neq0\\) (the sunny condition excludes the slope \\(-1\\)), the integer \\(\\frac{u+v}{v}\\) is a non‑zero integer. Hence from (2) we obtain that the difference of the sums \\(s_2-s_1\\) is a non‑zero multiple of \\(|u+v|/|v|\\ge 1\\).  \n\n   The possible values of the sum \\(s=a+b\\) for points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are the integers\n   \\[\n   2,3,\\dots ,n+1,\n   \\]\n   i.e. exactly \\(n\\) different numbers. Since two points on the same sunny line must have distinct sums, at most one point of each possible sum can lie on \\(\\ell\\). Consequently \\(\\ell\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). ∎\n\n3. **Necessary condition on \\(k\\).**  \n   Suppose we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n   Each sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) incidences by Lemma 1, while each non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) can contain at most \\(n+1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (the whole set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies in the strip \\(1\\le x\\le n,\\;1\\le y\\le n\\)). Hence the total number of incidences is at most\n   \\[\n   k\\cdot n+(n-k)\\cdot (n+1)=n^2+n-k .\n   \\]\n   Since we must cover \\(|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points, we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^2+n-k\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le\\frac{n}{2}.\n   \\]\n   Because \\(k\\) is an integer, we have the necessary bound\n   \\[\n   k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .                     \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Construction for every \\(k\\) satisfying (3).**  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) and put\n   \\[\n   m=n-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of non‑sunny lines}).\n   \\]\n   We use three families of lines:\n\n   *Sunny lines* (slope \\(+1\\)):\n   \\[\n   S_c:\\; y = x + c ,\\qquad c=0,1,\\dots ,k-1 .                \\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Vertical non‑sunny lines*:\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\; x=j ,\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{m}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{4.2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Horizontal non‑sunny lines*:\n   \\[\n   H_j:\\; y=j ,\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{4.3}\n   \\]\n\n   The total number of lines is\n   \\[\n   k+\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{m}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =k+m=n .\n   \\]\n   Exactly the first \\(k\\) lines are sunny.\n\n5. **Verification that every \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines (4.1)–(4.3).**  \n   Set \\(d=b-a\\).\n\n   *Case 1: \\(0\\le d\\le k-1\\).*  \n   Then \\(b-a=d\\) and the point belongs to the sunny line \\(S_d\\).\n\n   *Case 2: \\(d\\ge k\\).*  \n   From \\(a+b\\le n+1=k+m+1\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   a\\le\\frac{n+1-d}{2}\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\n   =\\frac{m+1}{2}\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{m}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(a\\) is one of the indices used in (4.2) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n   *Case 3: \\(d\\le -1\\).*  \n   Write \\(d=-t\\) with \\(t\\ge1\\). Then \\(b=a-t\\). Using \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we get\n   \\[\n   2a-t\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a\\le\\frac{n+1+t}{2}.\n   \\]\n   Because \\(t\\ge1\\) and \\(k\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\), we have \\(t\\le n-k\\le m\\). Consequently\n   \\[\n   a\\le\\frac{n+1+m}{2}= \\frac{k+2m+1}{2}\\le m .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(b=a-t\\le a-1\\le m-1\\). In particular\n   \\[\n   b\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\n   \\]\n   because \\(m\\ge 2\\) (recall \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) implies \\(m=n-k\\ge n-\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n}{2}\\ge 2\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\)). Therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\)