oincide,\n   contradicting their distinctness.  \n   Hence **different points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lying on the same sunny line\n   must have different values of the sum \\(a+b\\).**  \n\n   The possible sums for points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are the integers\n   \\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\); there are exactly \\(n\\) of them.  Therefore a sunny line\n   can contain at most one point for each sum, i.e. at most \\(n\\) points of\n   \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n3. **Lemma 2 (Points on a non‑sunny line).**  \n   If a line \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal, vertical or has slope \\(-1\\), then  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\ell\\cap\\mathcal P_n|\\le n .\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   *Horizontal* line \\(y=t\\) (\\(1\\le t\\le n\\)) meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) at the points\n   \\((a,t)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-t\\); the interval contains at most \\(n\\)\n   integers.  \n   *Vertical* line \\(x=s\\) is analogous.  \n   *Slope \\(-1\\)* line \\(x+y=s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)) meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) at the\n   points \\((a,s-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le s-1\\); again at most \\(n\\) such points. ∎\n\n\n\n4. **Necessary bound on \\(k\\).**  \n   Let a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of\n   \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   By Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) incidences, and by\n   Lemma 2 each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) incidences.  Hence the\n   total number of incidences is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\cdot n+(n-k)\\cdot n=n^{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since we must cover \\(|\\mathcal P_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) points,  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^{2}\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n   k\\le\\frac{n}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(k\\) is an integer,\n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n\n\n5. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   Put  \n\n   \\[\n   m=n-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of non‑sunny lines}).\n   \\]\n\n   We use three families of lines.\n\n   *Sunny lines* (slope \\(+1\\)):\n   \\[\n   S_{c}:\\; y = x + c ,\\qquad c=0,1,\\dots ,k-1 . \\tag{5.1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Vertical non‑sunny lines*:\n   \\[\n   V_{i}:\\; x=i ,\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{m}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{5.2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Horizontal non‑sunny lines*:\n   \\[\n   H_{j}:\\; y=j ,\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{m}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{5.3}\n   \\]\n\n   (If \\(m\\) is odd the last horizontal line coincides with the last vertical\n   one; the total number of distinct lines is still \\(k+m=n\\).)\n\n   Exactly the first \\(k\\) lines are sunny, the remaining \\(m\\) lines are\n   non‑sunny.\n\n\n\n6. **Verification that every \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of the\n   lines (5.1)–(5.3).**  \n\n   Set \\(d=b-a\\).\n\n   *Case 1: \\(|d|<k\\).*  \n   Then \\(d\\) is one of the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\) or \\(-1,-2,\\dots ,-(k-1)\\).  \n   If \\(0\\le d\\le k-1\\) the point satisfies \\(b-a=d\\) and belongs to the sunny\n   line \\(S_{d}\\).  \n   If \\(-k+1\\le d\\le -1\\) we write \\(d=-c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le k-1\\); then\n   \\(a-b=c\\) and the point lies on the vertical line \\(V_{a}\\) because,\n   as shown below, \\(a\\le\\lceil m/2\\rceil\\).\n\n   *Case 2: \\(|d|\\ge k\\).*  \n   Suppose first \\(d\\ge k\\).  From \\(a+b\\le n+1=k+m+1\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   2a+d\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n   a\\le\\frac{n+1-d}{2}\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\n   =\\frac{m+1}{2}\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{m}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the integer \\(a\\) is one of the indices used in (5.2), and the point\n   lies on the vertical line \\(V_{a}\\).\n\n   If \\(d\\le -k\\) we write \\(d=-c\\) with \\(c\\ge k\\).  Then \\(a-b=c\\) and\n\n   \\[\n   2b+c\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n   b\\le\\frac{n+1-c}{2}\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\n   =\\frac{m+1}{2}\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{m}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus the integer \\(b\\) is one of the indices used in (5.3), and the point\n   lies on the horizontal line \\(H_{b}\\).\n\n   The three possibilities for \\(d\\) are exhaustive, and in each case the\n   point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.  All lines\n   are distinct, and exactly \\(k\\) of them have slope \\(+1\\) (the sunny lines).\n\n\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (4) shows that any feasible configuration must satisfy\n   \\(k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n   The explicit construction above demonstrates that for every integer\n   \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\) such a configuration\n   exists.  \n\n   Therefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;}.\n   \\] ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
