s at most \\(n^{2}\\).  \n   Because \\(|\\mathcal P_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^{2}\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\), this does not by itself restrict \\(k\\).  \n   The restriction comes from the fact that a sunny line can occupy at most one point on each diagonal, whereas a diagonal \\(a+b=s\\) contains \\(s-1\\) points.  \n   To cover the \\(s-1\\) points of a diagonal we need at least \\(s-1\\) distinct lines that intersect that diagonal.  \n   A non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\)) can intersect a given diagonal in at most one point, while a sunny line can also intersect it in at most one point.  \n   Therefore, for each diagonal we need at least \\(\\lceil (s-1)/2\\rceil\\) lines of the whole family.  \n   Summing over all diagonals \\(s=2,\\dots ,n+1\\) gives a total requirement of  \n   \\[\n   \\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{s-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor +\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =n .\n   \\]\n   Since we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, the only way to meet the requirement is that on each diagonal at most one of the intersecting lines is sunny.  \n   Consequently the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the number of diagonals on which a sunny line is used, i.e.  \n   \\[\n   k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{★}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and put \\(m=n-k\\).  \n   We use three families of lines:\n\n   *Sunny lines* (slope \\(+1\\)):\n   \\[\n   S_{c}:\\; y = x + c ,\\qquad c=0,1,\\dots ,k-1 .\n   \\tag{5.1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Vertical lines*:\n   \\[\n   V_{i}:\\; x=i ,\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{m}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\tag{5.2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Horizontal lines*:\n   \\[\n   H_{j}:\\; y=j ,\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{m}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\tag{5.3}\n   \\]\n\n   If \\(m\\) is odd, the last vertical line coincides w