inct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \n   Let the remaining \\(t=n-k\\) lines be non\u2011sunny.  \n   A non\u2011sunny line can be of three possible slopes: \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\).  \n   * A line of slope \\(-1\\) coincides with a diagonal and therefore covers **all** points of that diagonal, i.e. \\(s-1\\) points for some \\(s\\).  \n   * A line of slope \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\) meets each diagonal in at most one point, so by Lemma\u202f1 it contains at most \\(n\\) points.  \n\n   To **minimise** the total number of points that must be supplied by the sunny lines we let every non\u2011sunny line be of slope \\(-1\\) and we choose the diagonals with the largest cardinalities, namely  \n   \\[\n   D_{n+1},D_{n},\\dots ,D_{k+2}.\n   \\]\n   (There are exactly \\(t=n-k\\) such diagonals.)  \n   Their total number of points is\n   \\[\n   S_t=\\sum_{s=k+2}^{n+1}(s-1)\n        =\\sum_{i=k+1}^{n}(i)\n        =\\frac{(k+1+n)(n-k)}2 .\n   \\tag{5.1}\n   \\]\n\n   The remaining points that have to be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines are therefore\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal P_n|-S_t\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{(k+1+n)(n-k)}2\n   =\\frac{n\\bigl(n+1-2k\\bigr)}2 .\n   \\tag{5.2}\n   \\]\n\n   Each sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points (Lemma\u202f3), hence we must have\n   \\[\n   k\\cdot n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n\\bigl(n+1-2k\\bigr)}2 .\n   \\]\n   Cancelling the factor \\(n>0\\) and rearranging gives the quadratic inequality\n   \\[\n   k^2-(2n+1)k+2n\\le0 .\n   \\tag{5.3}\n   \\]\n   The left\u2011hand side factors as \\((k-1)(k-2n)\\).  Because \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), inequality (5.3) is satisfied for **every** integer \\(k\\) with\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n .\n   \\tag{5.4}\n   \\]\n   Thus the only unavoidable restriction is the obvious one \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n6. **Explicit construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *Sunny lines* (slope \\(+1\\)):\n   \\[\n   S_c:\\; y = x + c ,\\qquad c=