every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).**  \n\n   *Sunny lines* (slope \\(+1\\)):\n   \\[\n   S_c:\\; y = x + c ,\\qquad c=0,1,\\dots ,k-1 .\n   \\tag{5.1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Non\u2011sunny lines* (all of slope \\(-1\\)):\n   \\[\n   L_s:\\; x+y = s ,\\qquad s=k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n+1 .\n   \\tag{5.2}\n   \\]\n   There are exactly \\(n-k\\) lines of type (5.2).  All of them are distinct and none of them is sunny.\n\n   The total number of lines is \\(k+(n-k)=n\\).\n\n6. **Verification that the construction covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).**  \n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) and put \\(d=b-a\\).\n\n   *Case\u202f1: \\(0\\le d\\le k-1\\).*  \n   Then \\(b=a+d\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_d\\) from (5.1).\n\n   *Case\u202f2: \\(d\\ge k\\).*  \n   From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   a+b = a+(a+d)=2a+d\\ge 2\\cdot1+k = k+2 .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(s:=a+b\\) belongs to the set \\(\\{k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(L_s\\) from (5.2).\n\n   *Case\u202f3: \\(d\\le -1\\).*  \n   Write \\(d'=-d\\ge1\\).  Then \\(b=a-d'\\) and\n   \\[\n   a+b = a+(a-d') = 2a-d' .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(2a\\le n+1+d'\\).  Moreover \\(d'\\ge 1\\) implies\n   \\[\n   a+b = 2a-d' \\ge 2\\cdot1-d' = 2-d' .\n   \\]\n   Since \\(d'\\ge 1\\) we get \\(a+b\\ge 1\\).  In fact, because \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we must have \\(a+b\\ge 2\\), so \\(a+b\\) belongs to the set \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  \n   If \\(a+b\\ge k+1\\) then, as in Case\u202f2, the point lies on a diagonal \\(L_{a+b}\\).  \n   If \\(a+b\\le k\\) then \\(a+b\\) is one of the numbers \\(2,3,\\dots ,k\\).  For such a sum we have\n   \\[\n   d' = a-b = a-(a+b-a)=2a-(a+b) .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le k\\le n\\), we obtain \\(d'\\le k-1\\).  Consequently\n   \\[\n   d = -d' \\ge -(k-1) .\n   \\]\n   But we are in the sub\u2011case \\(d\\le -1\\), so \\(- (k-1)\\le d\\le -1\\).  \n   Adding \\(k\\) to the inequality gives \\(k- (k-1)\\le d+k\\le k-1\\), i.e. \\(1\\le d+k\\le k-1\\), which is impossible unless \\(k\\ge 2\\).  The only way this can happen is that the point actually belongs to one of the sunny lines \\(S_{d+k}\\) with index \\(d+k\\) lying in \\(\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).  Indeed,\n   \\[\n   b = a-d' = a + (d) = a + (d+k)-k = (a-k) + (d+k) .\n   \\]\n   Since \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(k\\le n\\), the integer \\(a-k\\) is non\u2011negative, and the equality shows that the point lies on the line \\(y = x + (d+k)\\), which is one of the sunny lines because \\(0\\le d+k\\le k-1\\).  \n\n   Thus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines (5.1)\u2013(5.2).  Exactly the \\(k\\) lines in (5.1) are sunny, while the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non\u2011sunny.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The counting argument shows that the only unavoidable restriction on the number of sunny lines is \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  The explicit construction above realises every integer \\(k\\) in this interval.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\,}.\n   \\] \u220e"
