 \n   *Proof.* By Lemma\u202f1 a line meets each diagonal \\(D_s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)) in at most one point. Since there are exactly \\(n\\) diagonals, the total number of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that can lie on the line is at most \\(n\\). Moreover the intersections with distinct diagonals are distinct points because different diagonals consist of points with different sums of coordinates. \u220e\n\n4. **Counting the points that must be supplied by sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny. Let the remaining \\(t=n-k\\) lines be *non\u2011sunny* (horizontal, vertical, or parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\n   *Claim.* Among all families of \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines, the greatest possible number of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that they can cover is obtained by taking the \\(t\\) diagonals \\(D_{k+2},D_{k+3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\).  \n\n   *Proof of the claim.*  \n   \u2013 Any non\u2011sunny line that is not a diagonal can contain at most one point from each diagonal (Lemma\u202f1) and, because it is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), it can intersect at most one diagonal in more than one point only if it coincides with that diagonal, which we have excluded. Hence such a line can contain at most one point from each of the \\(n\\) diagonals, i.e. at most \\(n\\) points in total.  \n   \u2013 A diagonal \\(D_s\\) itself contains exactly \\(|D_s|=s-1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). The numbers \\(|D_s|\\) increase with \\(s\\). Therefore, to maximise the total number of covered points with exactly \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines, we should select those diagonals with the largest cardinalities, namely the \\(t\\) diagonals having the largest indices. Since the indices run from \\(2\\) to \\(n+1\\), the set of largest indices of size \\(t\\) is \\(\\{k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) (recall \\(t=n-k\\)). \u220e\n\n   Using the claim, the number of points covered by the chosen non\u2011sunny diagonals is\n   \\[\n   S_t=\\sum_{s=k+2}^{n+1}(s-1)\n        =\\sum_{i=k+1}^{n}i\n        =\\frac{(k+1+n)(n-k)}2 .\n   \\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n   Consequently the number of points that remain uncovered and must be supplied by the \\(k\\) sunny lines equals\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal P_n|-S_t\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{(k+1+n)(n-k)}2\n   =\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{4.2}\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma\u202f2 each sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); therefore we need\n   \\[\n   k\\cdot n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   For \\(k=0\\) the inequality is trivial. For \\(k>0\\) we divide by \\(k\\) and obtain\n   \\[\n   n\\ge\\frac{k+1}{2}\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad k\\le 2n-1 .\n   \\tag{4.3}\n   \\]\n   Since we have only \\(n\\) lines in total, we must also have \\(k\\le n\\). Combining (4.3) with this obvious bound yields the single necessary condition\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n .\n   \\tag{4.4}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   For a given integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) define  \n\n   *Sunny lines (slope \\(+1\\))*  \n   \\[\n   S_c:\\; y = x + c ,\\qquad c=0,1,\\dots ,k-1 .\n   \\tag{5.1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Non\u2011sunny lines (all of slope \\(-1\\))*  \n   \\[\n   L_s:\\; x+y = s ,\\qquad s=k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n   \\tag{5.2}\n   \\]\n   There are exactly \\(k\\) lines of type (5.1) and \\(n-k\\) lines of type (5.2); all are distinct, and the lines in (5.2) are not sunny because they are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n6. **Verification that the construction covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) and set \\(d=b-a\\).\n\n   *Case\u202f1: \\(0\\le d\\le k-1\\).*  \n   Then \\(b=a+d\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_d\\) from (5.1).\n\n   *Case\u202f2: \\(d\\ge k\\).*  \n   From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a+b\\ge k+2\\) (because \\(a\\ge1\\)). Hence \\(s:=a+b\\) belongs to the set \\(\\{k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(L_s\\) from (5.2).\n\n   *Case\u202f3: \\(d\\le -1\\).*  \n   Write \\(d=-\\delta\\) with \\(\\delta\\ge1\\). Then \\(b=a-\\delta\\) and\n   \\[\n   a+b = a+(a-\\delta)=2a-\\delta .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(2a\\le n+1+\\delta\\).  \n\n   \u2013 If \\(a+b\\ge k+2\\) we are again in Case\u202f2, so the point lies on a line \\(L_{a+b}\\).  \n\n   \u2013 If \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) then \\(a+b\\) is one of the integers \\(2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\). Since \\(\\delta\\ge1\\), the equality \\(d=b-a=-(2a-(a+b))\\) gives\n     \\[\n     d = (a+b)-2a .\n     \\]\n     The right\u2011hand side ranges over the integers \\(- (k-1),\\dots , -1\\) when \\(a\\) runs through the admissible positive values; consequently \\(d\\) belongs to \\(\\{-(k-1),\\dots ,-1\\}\\). Adding \\(k\\) we obtain an integer\n     \\[\n     c:=d+k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}.\n     \\]\n     Then\n     \\[\n     b = a+d = a+(c-k)= (a-k)+c .\n     \\]\n     Since \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(k\\le n\\), the equality \\(b=a+c\\) holds, i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_c\\) with \\(c\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).\n\n   In every possible situation the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one of the lines (5.1)\u2013(5.2). Hence the family of \\(n\\) lines constructed above indeed covers the whole set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The counting argument forces only the condition \\(0\\le k\\le n\\). The explicit construction shows that for each integer \\(k\\) in this interval there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines satisfying the covering requirement. Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n } .\n   \\] \u220e"
