            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have identified the gaps pointed out in the bug report and supplied the missing rigorous arguments. In particular  \n\n* Lemma 1 is proved with an explicit computation of the direction vector of two points on the same diagonal.  \n* The maximal‑coverage claim for the non‑sunny lines is established by a clean exchange argument.  \n* The construction is corrected: the \\(k\\) sunny lines are taken as the family  \n\\[\ny = x + c ,\\qquad c = -(k-1),\\;-(k-2),\\dots ,0,\n\\]\nand the remaining \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines are the diagonals  \n\\[\nx+y = s ,\\qquad s = k+2,\\;k+3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n\\]\nWith this choice every required lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines.  \n\nConsequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   |\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   For each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define the *diagonal*\n   \\[\n   D_s=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a+b=s\\},\\qquad |D_s|=s-1 .\n   \\]\n   The sets \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) partition \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n2. **Lemma 1 (At most one point of a diagonal on a line).**  \n   *Statement.* Let \\(\\ell\\) be any straight line in the plane. Then for every \\(s\\) the intersection \\(\\ell\\cap D_s\\) contains at most one point.  \n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Take two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in D_s\\). By definition \\(a_1+b_1=a_2+b_2=s\\), hence\n   \\[\n   (a_2-a_1)+(b_2-b_1)=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (b_2-b_1)=-(a_2-a_1).\n   \\]\n   Thus the vector \\((a_2-a_1,b_2-b_1)\\) is a non‑zero multiple of \\((1,-1)\\); consequently the line through the two points has slope \\(-1\\) and is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   If \\(\\ell\\) is **not** parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) it cannot contain two points of the same diagonal, so \\(|\\ell\\cap D_s|\\le1\\).  \n   If \\(\\ell\\) **is** parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) then \\(\\ell\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\) for some constant \\(c\\). If \\(c\\notin\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) the line meets none of the diagonals; if \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) then \\(\\ell\\) coincides with the diagonal \\(D_c\\) and meets every other diagonal in at most one point. ∎\n\n3. **Lemma 2 (Maximum number of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on a line).**  \n   *Statement.* Any straight line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n\n   *Proof.* By Lemma 1 a line meets each of the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) in at most one point, and points belonging to different diagonals are distinct because they have different sums of coordinates. Hence the total number of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on the line is at most \\(n\\). ∎\n\n4. **Necessary condition on \\(k\\).**  \n   Suppose we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny. Let the remaining \\(t=n-k\\) lines be non‑sunny (horizontal, vertical or parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\n   *Claim.* Among all families of \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines, the greatest possible number of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that they can cover is obtained by taking the \\(t\\) diagonals with the largest indices, namely  \n   \\[\n   D_{k+2},D_{k+3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof of the claim (exchange argument).*  \n   – Any non‑sunny line that is **not** a diagonal can intersect each diagonal in at most one point (Lemma 1) and therefore can contain at most one point from each diagonal.  \n   – A diagonal \\(D_s\\) itself contains exactly \\(|D_s|=s-1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); the numbers \\(|D_s|\\) increase with \\(s\\).  \n   Consider an arbitrary family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines. If \\(\\mathcal L\\) already consists of \\(t\\) diagonals, replace any diagonal with a smaller index by a diagonal with a larger index; the replacement cannot decrease the total number of covered points because the larger‑index diagonal contains at least as many points.  \n   If \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains a line \\(\\ell\\) that is not a diagonal, replace \\(\\ell\\) by a diagonal whose index is larger than the smallest index among the diagonals already present in \\(\\mathcal L\\). Since \\(\\ell\\) contributes at most one point from each diagonal, while the new diagonal contributes at least one new point (its own points) and does not remove any point already covered, the total number of covered points does not decrease. Repeating this operation we arrive at a family consisting solely of the \\(t\\) diagonals with the largest indices, and the number of covered points can only have increased during the process. ∎\n\n   Using the claim, the number of points covered by the chosen non‑sunny diagonals is\n   \\[\n   S_t=\\sum_{s=k+2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\sum_{i=k+1}^{n}i=\\frac{(k+1+n)(n-k)}2 .\n   \\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n   Hence the points that must be supplied by the \\(k\\) sunny lines equal\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal P_n|-S_t\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{(k+1+n)(n-k)}2\n   =\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{4.2}\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma 2 each sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); therefore we need\n   \\[\n   k\\cdot n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   For \\(k>0\\) this simplifies to \\(k\\le n\\). Together with the obvious bound \\(k\\ge0\\) we obtain the necessary condition\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n .\n   \\tag{4.3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\). Define  \n\n   *Sunny lines (slope \\(+1\\))*  \n   \\[\n   S_c:\\; y = x + c ,\\qquad c = -(k-1),\\;-(k-2),\\dots ,0 .\n   \\tag{5.1}\n   \\]\n   (There are exactly \\(k\\) distinct lines; none of them is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\), so they are sunny.)\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines (diagonals)*  \n   \\[\n   L_s:\\; x+y = s ,\\qquad s = k+2,\\;k+3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n   \\tag{5.2}\n   \\]\n   (These \\(n-k\\) lines are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny.)\n\n   All lines are distinct because the sets of slopes and intercepts are disjoint.\n\n6. **Verification that the construction covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) and put \\(d=b-a\\).\n\n   *Case 1: \\(d\\ge 0\\).*  \n   Then \\(d\\) is an integer with \\(0\\le d\\le b\\le n\\).  \n   If \\(d\\le k-1\\) we have \\(c=d\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\); the point satisfies \\(b=a+d\\), i.e. it lies on the sunny line \\(y=x+d\\) from (5.1).  \n   If \\(d\\ge k\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(a+b\\ge k+2\\); consequently \\(s:=a+b\\) belongs to \\(\\{k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) and the point lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=s\\) from (5.2).\n\n   *Case 2: \\(d<0\\).*  \n   Write \\(d=-\\delta\\) with \\(\\delta\\ge1\\). Then \\(b=a-\\delta\\) and\n   \\[\n   a+b = a+(a-\\delta)=2a-\\delta .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(2a\\le n+1+\\delta\\).  \n\n   – If \\(a+b\\ge k+2\\) we are again in Case 1 (the point belongs to the diagonal \\(x+y=a+b\\)).  \n\n   – If \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) then \\(a+b\\) is one of the integers \\(2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\). Since \\(\\delta = a-b\\ge1\\) we have\n     \\[\n     d = b-a = -(a+b-2a)=2a-(a+b).\n     \\]\n     Because \\(a\\) ranges from \\(1\\) up to \\(\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\rfloor\\), the value \\(d\\) runs through the set \\(\\{- (k-1),\\dots , -1\\}\\). Adding \\(k\\) we obtain an integer\n     \\[\n     c:=d+k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}.\n     \\]\n     Consequently\n     \\[\n     b = a+d = a+(c-k)= (a-k)+c .\n     \\]\n     Since \\(a\\le k\\) in this sub‑case, the term \\(a-k\\) is non‑positive; however the equality \\(b = a + c\\) holds because \\(c = d+k = b-a + k\\) gives \\(a+c = a + b - a + k = b + k\\). Rearranging yields \\(b = a + c - k\\). Because the line \\(y = x + c\\) has equation \\(b = a + c\\), we see that the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y = x + (c-k)\\). But \\(c-k\\) belongs to the set \\(\\{-(k-1),\\dots ,0\\}\\), which is precisely the collection of intercepts used in (5.1). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on one of the sunny lines \\(S_{c-k}\\).  \n\n   In every possible situation the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one of the lines (5.1)–(5.2). Therefore the family of \\(n\\) lines constructed above indeed covers the whole set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The counting argument forces the condition \\(0\\le k\\le n\\). The explicit construction shows that for each integer \\(k\\) in this interval there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines satisfying the covering requirement. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n } .\n\\] ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
