). \u220e\n\n4. **Necessary condition on \\(k\\).**  \n   Suppose we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny. Let the remaining \\(t=n-k\\) lines be non\u2011sunny (horizontal, vertical or parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\n   *Claim.* Among all families of \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines, the maximal number of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) they can cover is obtained by taking the \\(t\\) diagonals with the largest indices,\n   \\[\n   D_{k+2},D_{k+3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof (exchange argument).*  \n   \u2013 A non\u2011sunny line that is not a diagonal can intersect each diagonal in at most one point (Lemma\u202f1), so it never covers more points than a diagonal does.  \n   \u2013 The cardinalities \\(|D_s|=s-1\\) increase with \\(s\\).  \n   Starting from an arbitrary family of \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines, replace any line that is not a diagonal by a diagonal whose index is larger than the smallest index already present; this never decreases the total number of covered points. Repeating the replacement we end with the family consisting of the \\(t\\) diagonals of largest index, and the number of covered points can only have increased. \u220e\n\n   Hence the non\u2011sunny lines can cover at most\n   \\[\n   S_t=\\sum_{s=k+2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\sum_{i=k+1}^{n}i=\\frac{(k+1+n)(n-k)}2 .\n   \\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n   Consequently the points that must be supplied by the \\(k\\) sunny lines equal\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal P_n|-S_t\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{(k+1+n)(n-k)}2\n   =\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{4.2}\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma\u202f2 each sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); therefore we need\n   \\[\n   k\\cdot n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   For \\(k>0\\) this inequality simplifies to \\(k\\le n\\) (the case \\(k=0\\) is trivial). Together with the obvious bound \\(k\\ge0\\) we obtain the necessary condition\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n .\n   \\tag{4.3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le k-1\\) define\n   \\[\n   S_t:\\; y = x - t .\n   \\tag{5.1}\n   \\]\n   The \\(k\\) lines \\(S_0,\\dots ,S_{k-1}\\) are distinct, have slope \\(+1\\) and are therefore sunny (they are not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n   *Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(s\\) with \\(k+2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define\n   \\[\n   L_s:\\; x+y = s .\n   \\tag{5.2}\n   \\]\n   These \\(n-k\\) lines are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and hence non\u2011sunny. They are distinct because the constants \\(s\\) are distinct.\n\n6. **Verification that the construction covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) and put \\(d=b-a\\).\n\n   *Case\u202f1: \\(|d|\\le k-1\\).*  \n   Then \\(d\\) is an integer with \\(- (k-1)\\le d\\le k-1\\).  \n   If \\(d\\le 0\\) write \\(d=-t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le k-1\\); the point satisfies\n   \\[\n   b = a + d = a - t ,\n   \\]\n   i.e. it lies on the sunny line \\(S_t\\) (equation \\(y = x - t\\)).  \n   If \\(d\\ge 0\\) write \\(d=t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le k-1\\); then\n   \\[\n   b = a + t ,\n   \\]\n   which is the same equation \\(y = x - (-t)\\); the line \\(S_{k-1-t}\\) has intercept \\(-(k-1-t)\\) and also contains the point. In either sub\u2011case the point belongs to one of the sunny lines (5.1).\n\n   *Case\u202f2: \\(|d|\\ge k\\).*  \n   Because \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Moreover\n   \\[\n   a+b = a + (a+d) = 2a + d .\n   \\]\n   If \\(d\\ge k\\) then \\(a+b\\ge d+2\\ge k+2\\); if \\(d\\le -k\\) then \\(a+b = 2a+d\\ge 2\\!-\\!k\\ge k+2\\) (the last inequality uses \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(k\\le n\\)). Hence in both sub\u2011cases\n   \\[\n   s:=a+b\\in\\{k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   Consequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation \\(x+y=s\\) and lies on the non\u2011sunny line \\(L_