2-a_1)+(b_2-b_1)=0 .\n   \\]\n   Hence the direction vector \\((a_2-a_1,b_2-b_1)\\) is a non‑zero multiple of \\((1,-1)\\); the line through the two points is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\). Therefore a line that is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) can meet a diagonal in at most one point. ∎\n\n3. **Lemma 2 (maximal number of points on a line).**  \n   *Statement.* Any line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n\n   *Proof.*  \n   – If the line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) it coincides with some diagonal \\(D_s\\); the largest diagonal is \\(D_{n+1}\\) and \\(|D_{n+1}|=n\\).  \n   – If the line is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), Lemma 1 shows that it meets each diagonal \\(D_s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)) in at most one point. Since there are exactly \\(n\\) diagonals, the total number of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on the line is at most \\(n\\). ∎\n\n4. **Necessary condition on \\(k\\).**  \n   Assume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny. Let the remaining \\(t=n-k\\) lines be non‑sunny (i.e. horizontal, vertical or parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\n   *Claim.* Among all families of \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines, the maximal number of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) they can cover is obtained by taking the \\(t\\) diagonals with the largest indices,\n   \\[\n   D_{k+1},D_{k+2},\\dots ,D_{n+1}.\n   \\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof of the claim (exchange argument).*  \n   Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be any family of \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n   – If a line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) but is not a diagonal, then it meets each diagonal in at most one point (Lemma 1). Replacing it by the diagonal of the *largest* index that is not already used can only increase (or keep) the number of points covered, because the diagonal \\(D_s\\) contains \\(s-1\\) points, which is at least as many as the former line can meet (at most one point per diagonal, and there are \\(n\\) diagonals).  \n   – If a line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) or vertical (\\(x=c\\)), then it meets diagonal \\(D_s\\) in at most one point for each \\(s\\). Again we may replace it by a diagonal of larger index without decreasing the total coverage.  \n\n   Repeating this replacement while preserving distinctness of the lines yields a family consisting solely of diagonals, and because each replacement never decreases the number of covered points, the maximal possible coverage is achieved when the diagonals of largest size are chosen, i.e. (4.1). ∎\n\n   The number of points covered by the diagonals (4.1) is\n   \\[\n   S_t=\\sum_{s=k+1}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\sum_{i=k}^{n}i\n        =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{(k-1)k}2 .\n   \\tag{4.2}\n   \\]\n   Hence the points that must be supplied by the \\(k\\) sunny lines equal\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal P_n|-S_t\n   =\\frac{(k-1)k}2 .\n   \\tag{4.3}\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma 2 each sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); therefore we need\n   \\[\n   k\\cdot n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{(k-1)k}2 .\n   \\]\n   For \\(k>0\\) this simplifies to\n   \\[\n   n\\ge\\frac{k-1}2\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad k\\le 2n+1 .\n   \\]\n   Since we have only \\(n\\) lines in total, the stronger bound \\(k\\le n\\) follows. Together with the obvious bound \\(k\\ge0\\) we obtain the necessary condition\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n .\n   \\tag{4.4}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(0\\le c\\le k-1\\) define\n   \\[\n   S_c:\\; y = x + c .\n   \\tag{5.1}\n   \\]\n   The \\(k\\) lines \\(S_0,S_1,\\dots ,S_{k-1}\\) are distinct, have slope \\(+1\\) and are therefore sunny (they are not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(s\\) with \\(k+1\\le s\\le n+1\\) define\n   \\[\n   L_s:\\; x+y = s .\n   \\tag{5.2}\n   \\]\n   These \\(n-k\\) lines are pa