ent strictly increases the sum of the indices of the chosen diagonals, the process must terminate, and the final family consists solely of diagonals. Moreover, at termination we have chosen the \\(t\\) diagonals with the largest possible indices, i.e. the family (4.1). ∎\n\n   The number of points covered by the diagonals (4.1) is\n   \\[\n   S_t=\\sum_{s=n-t+2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\sum_{i=n-t+1}^{n}i\n        =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{(n-t)(n-t+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{4.2}\n   \\]\n   Hence the points that must be supplied by the \\(k\\) sunny lines equal\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal P_n|-S_t=\\frac{(n-t)(n-t+1)}2\n                     =\\frac{(k-1)k}2 .\n   \\tag{4.3}\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma 2 each sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); therefore we need\n   \\[\n   k\\cdot n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{(k-1)k}2 .\n   \\]\n   For \\(k>0\\) this inequality is always satisfied because \\(n\\ge 1\\). The only restriction comes from the fact that we have only \\(n\\) lines in total, so \\(k\\le n\\). Together with the obvious bound \\(k\\ge0\\) we obtain the necessary condition\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n .\n   \\tag{4.4}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   *Sunny lines.* For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(0\\le c\\le k-1\\) define\n   \\[\n   S_c:\\; y = x + c .\n   \\tag{5.1}\n   \\]\n   The \\(k\\) lines \\(S_0,S_1,\\dots ,S_{k-1}\\) are distinct, have slope \\(+1\\) and are therefore sunny (they are not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\n   *Non‑sunny lines.* For each integer \\(s\\) with \\(k+1\\le s\\le n+1\\) define\n   \\[\n   L_s:\\; x+y = s .\n   \\tag{5.2}\n   \\]\n   These \\(n-k\\) lines are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and hence non‑sunny. They are distinct because the constants \\(s\\) are distinct.\n\n6. **Verification that the construction covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) and put \\(d=b-a\\).\n\n   *Case 1: \\(0\\le d\\le k-1\\).*  \n   Then \\(b=a+d\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of the sunny line \\(S_d\\) (5.1). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on a sunny line.\n\n   *Case 2: \\(d\\le -1\\).*  \n   Here \\(b<a\\). Because \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Moreover,\n   \\[\n   a+b = a + (a+d) = 2a + d .\n   \\]\n   Since \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(d\\le -1\\),\n   \\[\n   a+b \\ge 2\\cdot 1 + d \\ge 2 - (k-1) .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(k\\le n\\), the right‑hand side is at most \\(n+1\\); more importantly, the integer\n   \\[\n   s:=a+b\n   \\]\n   satisfies \\(k+1\\le s\\le n+1\\). Indeed, if \\(s\\le k\\) then\n   \\[\n   2a+d = s \\le k \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a \\le \\frac{k-d}{2}\\le \\frac{k+1}{2},\n   \\]\n   which forces \\(a\\le k\\). But then \\(b=a+d\\le a-1\\le k-1\\), contradicting \\(d\\le -1\\) together with the definition of the sunny lines (they already cover all points with \\(b-a\\ge 0\\)). Hence \\(s\\ge k+1\\). Consequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation \\(x+y=s\\) and lies on the non‑sunny line \\(L_s\\) defined in (5.2).\n\n   The two cases exhaust all possibilities for the difference \\(d=b-a\\); therefore every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines (5.1)–(5.2). The family consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, all distinct, as required.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The counting argument forces \\(0\\le k\\le n\\). The explicit construction above shows that for each integer \\(k\\) in this interval there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n } .\n\\] ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
