e \\(n=5,\\;k=4\\) and the point \\((2,1)\\). Here \\(d=-1\\) and \\(s=3\\), which is **less** than \\(k+1=5\\). Consequently \\((2,1)\\) is not on any line \\(L_s\\) (since those start at \\(s=5\\)) and also not on any sunny line (since \\(d\\) is negative). Hence the construction fails to cover this point. The same failure occurs for any \\(k> \\lceil n/2\\rceil\\). Therefore the claimed construction does **not** work for all \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n]\\). This is a **Critical Error**.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f6 \u2013 Conclusion\nThe final statement \u201cthe set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\)\u201d is **incorrectly justified**: the necessary condition was derived from a flawed algebraic step and an unproved maximality claim, and the explicit construction does not actually cover all required points for many values of \\(k\\). Hence the solution does not constitute a valid proof."

