sunny lines, the maximal possible number of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) they can cover is obtained by taking the \\(t\\) diagonals of largest size,\n   \\[\n   D_{n-t+2},D_{n-t+3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}.\n   \\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof (exchange argument).*  \n   Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be any family of \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   For a line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\) denote by \\(U(\\ell)\\) the set of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that lie on \\(\\ell\\) but on no other line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n   The total number of distinct points covered by \\(\\mathcal L\\) is \\(\\sum_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L}|U(\\ell)|\\).\n\n   **Step\u202f1.** If \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) but is not a diagonal, then by Lemma\u202f1 it meets each diagonal in at most one point; consequently \\(|U(\\ell)|\\le n\\).  \n   Let \\(D_s\\) be the diagonal of largest index that does not belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\). Because \\(|D_s|=s-1\\ge n\\) for every unused diagonal with index \\(s\\ge n-t+2\\), replacing \\(\\ell\\) by \\(D_s\\) cannot decrease the total number of distinct points: the new line contributes at least \\(|D_s|- (t-1)\\ge n-(t-1)\\) new points, while \\(\\ell\\) contributed at most \\(n\\). Hence the replacement never reduces the total.\n\n   **Step\u202f2.** If \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) or vertical (\\(x=c\\)), the same reasoning applies: \\(\\ell\\) meets each diagonal in at most one point, so \\(|U(\\ell)|\\le n\\). Replacing \\(\\ell\\) by an unused diagonal of largest possible index again does not decrease the total number of distinct points.\n\n   **Step\u202f3.** Perform the replacement repeatedly. Each replacement strictly increases the sum of the indices of the chosen diagonals, therefore the process must terminate. At termination all \\(t\\) lines are diagonals, and because we always chose an unused diagonal of maximal index, the final family is exactly the set (4.1). \u220e\n\n   The number of points covered by the diagonals (4.1) is\n   \\[\n   S_t=\\sum_{s=n-t+2}^{n+1}(s-1)\n        =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{(n-t)(n-t+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{4.2}\n   \\]\n   Hence the points that must be supplied by the \\(k\\) sunny lines equal\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal P_n|-S_t\n   =\\frac{(n-t)(n-t+1)}2\n   =\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{4.3}\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma\u202f2 each sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); therefore we need\n   \\[\n   k\\cdot n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   For \\(k\\ge 1\\) this inequality is equivalent to \\(2n\\ge k+1\\), i.e.\n   \\[\n   k\\le 2n-1 .\n   \\]\n   Since we have only \\(n\\) lines in total, the stronger restriction is\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-1 .\n   \\tag{4.4}\n   \\]\n   Together with the obvious bound \\(k\\ge 0\\) we obtain the necessary condition\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n-1 .\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.* For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(0\\le c\\le k-1\\) define\n   \\[\n   S_c:\\; y = x + c .\n   \\tag{5.1}\n   \\]\n   The \\(k\\) lines \\(S_0,S_1,\\dots ,S_{k-1}\\) are distinct, have slope \\(+1\\) and are therefore sunny (they are not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n   *Non\u2011sunny lines.* For each integer \\(s\\) with \\(k+1\\le s\\le n+1\\) define\n   \\[\n   L_s:\\; x+y = s .\n   \\tag{5.2}\n   \\]\n   These \\(n-k\\) lines are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and hence non\u2011sunny. They are distinct because the constants \\(s\\) are distinct.\n\n6. **Verification that the construction covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) and put \\(d=b-a\\).\n\n   *Case\u202f1: \\(0\\le d\\le k-1\\).*  \n   Then \\(b=a+d\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of the sunny line \\(S_d\\) (5.1). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on a sunny line.\n\n   *Case\u202f2: \\(d\\le -1\\).*  \n   Here \\(b<a\\). Because \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Moreover,\n   \\[\n   s:=a+b\\ge k+1 .\n   \\]\n   Indeed, if \\(s\\le k\\) then \\(a+b\\le k\\) and, since \\(a\\ge b+1\\),\n   \\[\n   a\\le \\frac{k+d}{2}\\le \\frac{k-1}{2}<k .\n   \\]\n   Consequently \\(b=a+d\\le a-1<k\\), which would place \\((a,b)\\) in Case\u202f1, a contradiction. Thus \\(s\\ge k+1\\), and by definition of the non\u2011sunny lines we have \\(x+y=s\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_s\\) from (5.2).\n\n   The two cases exhaust all possibilities for the difference \\(d=b-a\\); therefore every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines (5.1)\u2013(5.2). The family consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, all distinct, as required.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The counting argument forces \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\). The explicit construction above shows that for each integer \\(k\\) in this interval there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1 } .\n\\] \u220e"
