            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have identified the errors pointed out in the bug report and corrected them.  \nThe claim that the non‑sunny lines must be the largest diagonals is false, and consequently the derived bound \\(k\\le n-1\\) is invalid.  \nA correct necessary condition is simply \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) (the obvious bound that the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines).  \nI give a rigorous proof that **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realized, and that no other values are possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   |\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lemma 1 (Maximum points on a line).**  \n   Any line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n   *Proof.*  \n   – If the line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) it coincides with a diagonal \\(D_s=\\{a+b=s\\}\\); the largest diagonal is \\(D_{n+1}\\) and has exactly \\(n\\) points.  \n   – Otherwise the line meets each diagonal in at most one point (otherwise it would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)). There are \\(n\\) diagonals, hence at most \\(n\\) points. ∎\n\n3. **Necessary condition.**  \n   In a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines.  \n   Trivially \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) because there are only \\(n\\) lines.  \n   No stronger restriction follows from Lemma 1: the \\(k\\) sunny lines together can cover at most \\(kn\\) points, while \\(|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le kn\\) holds for every \\(k\\ge n\\) and also for \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\ge2\\). Hence the inequality does not eliminate any \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n]\\).  \n   Therefore the **only** necessary condition is  \n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n .\n   \\tag{3.1}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Construction for any admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.* For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(0\\le c\\le k-1\\) define  \n   \\[\n   S_c:\\; y = x + c .\n   \\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n   These \\(k\\) lines have slope \\(+1\\); they are not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor to \\(x+y=0\\), hence they are sunny and distinct.\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines.*  \n   - If \\(k<n\\), for each integer \\(s\\) with \\(k+1\\le s\\le n+1\\) define  \n     \\[\n     L_s:\\; x+y = s .\n     \\tag{4.2}\n     \\]\n     The \\(n-k\\) lines \\(L_s\\) are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (hence non‑sunny) and distinct.  \n   - If \\(k=n\\) there are no non‑sunny lines; we simply use the \\(n\\) sunny lines (4.1) with \\(c=0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\).\n\n5. **Verification that the construction covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) and set \\(d=b-a\\).\n\n   *Case 1: \\(0\\le d\\le k-1\\).*  \n   Then \\(b=a+d\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of the sunny line \\(S_d\\) (4.1). Hence the point lies on a sunny line.\n\n   *Case 2: \\(d\\le -1\\).*  \n   Here \\(b<a\\). Because \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Moreover,\n   \\[\n   a+b\\ge k+1 .\n   \\]\n   Indeed, if \\(a+b\\le k\\) then \\(a\\le k-1\\) and \\(b=a+d\\le a-1\\le k-2\\), which would give \\(d\\ge 0\\), contradicting \\(d\\le -1\\).  \n   Thus \\(s:=a+b\\) satisfies \\(k+1\\le s\\le n+1\\), and the point lies on the non‑sunny line \\(L_s\\) defined in (4.2).\n\n   The two cases exhaust all possibilities for the difference \\(d=b-a\\); consequently every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines (4.1)–(4.2). All lines are distinct, and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition (3.1) is also sufficient, as demonstrated by the explicit construction for each admissible \\(k\\). Hence the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\,}.\n\\] ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
