y lines.* For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(0\\le c\\le k-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_c:\\; y = x + c . \\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n\n   These \\(k\\) lines have slope \\(+1\\); they are not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor to \\(x+y=0\\), hence they are sunny and distinct.\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines.*  \n   We use the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines in a way that depends on the size of \\(k\\).\n\n   *Case A – \\(k\\ge \\displaystyle\\frac{n}{2}\\).*  \n   Define the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_t:\\; x = t ,\\qquad t=1,2,\\dots ,n-k . \\tag{4.2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Case B – \\(k< \\displaystyle\\frac{n}{2}\\).*  \n   Define the horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   H_t:\\; y = t ,\\qquad t=1,2,\\dots ,n-k . \\tag{4.3}\n   \\]\n\n   In both cases we obtain exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_c\\) are sunny.\n\n5. **Verification that the construction covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) and put \\(d=b-a\\).\n\n   *Step 1 – Points with \\(0\\le d\\le k-1\\).*  \n   Then \\(b=a+d\\) and the point satisfies the equation of the sunny line \\(S_d\\) (4.1). Hence it lies on a sunny line.\n\n   *Step 2 – Points with \\(d\\le -1\\) (i.e. \\(b<a\\)).*  \n\n   - **Case A (\\(k\\ge n/2\\)).**  \n     Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b<a\\),\n\n     \\[\n     a \\le n+1-b \\le n+1-(a-1)=n+2-a\\; \\Longrightarrow\\; a\\le n-k .\n     \\]\n\n     The last inequality follows from the assumption \\(k\\ge n/2\\):\n     \\[\n     a\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(a-1)=n+2-a\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;2a\\le n+2\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;a\\le\\frac{n}{2}+1\\le n-k .\n     \\]\n     Consequently the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (4.2) passes through \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   - **Case B (\\(k< n/2\\)).**  \n     Here \\(b<a\\) implies \\(b\\le n-k\\). Indeed,\n     \\[\n     b = (a+b)-a \\le (n+1)-a \\le (n+1)-(k+1)=n-k .\n     \\]\n     (The last inequality uses \\(a\\ge k+1\\), which holds because otherwise \\(d=b-a\\ge 0\\) and the point would already be covered by a sunny line.)  \n     Hence the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) (4.3) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   *Step 3 – Points with \\(d\\ge k\\).*  \n   Such a point would satisfy \\(b\\ge a+k\\). Adding the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives\n   \\[\n   2a+k\\le n+1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}.\n   \\]\n   Because \\(k\\ge 0\\), the right‑hand side is at most \\(n-k\\). Therefore \\(a\\le n-k\\) and the point is again covered by the vertical line \\(V_a\\) in Case A, or by the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) in Case B (since \\(b\\ge a+k\\ge k+1\\) forces \\(b\\le n-k\\) when \\(k<n/2\\)).  \n\n   The three steps exhaust all possibilities for \\((a,b)\\); consequently every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines defined in (4.1)–(4.3).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Condition (3.1) is necessary, and the explicit constructions above show that it is also sufficient. Hence the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n } .\n\\] ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
