\) is defined as \\(\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid b-a=-d\\}\\), i.e. the set of integer points on the line \\(b = a-d\\), which has **slope\u202f+1**, not \\(-1\\). Sunny lines are precisely those whose slope is **not** \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); a slope of \\(+1\\) is allowed. Consequently a sunny line *can* contain points of \\(D_{-d}\\) (indeed the construction later uses the sunny lines \\(y=x+c\\) to cover such points). The statement is false.\n\n**Classification:** Critical Error \u2013 the false claim invalidates the entire counting argument that follows.\n\n### 5. Consequence of the false claim \u2013 \u201cwe need at least one non\u2011sunny line for each distinct negative difference\u201d\nBecause the premise that sunny lines cannot cover any point with a negative difference is false, the deduction that each distinct negative difference forces a separate non\u2011sunny line is unjustified. The inequality\n\\[\nn-k\\ge\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\nand the resulting bound \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) are therefore **not proved**.\n\n**Classification:** Critical Error (directly dependent on the previous error).\n\n### 6. Construction (Step\u202f5)\nThe solution proposes, for a given \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\):\n\n* Sunny lines \\(S_c: y = x + c\\) for \\(c=0,\\dots ,k-1\\) (slope\u202f1).  \n* Vertical lines \\(V_t: x = t\\) for \\(t=1,\\dots ,n-2k\\).  \n* Horizontal lines \\(H_t: y = t\\) for \\(t=1,\\dots ,k\\).\n\nThe total number of lines is indeed \\(k+(n-2k)+k=n\\). The lines are distinct.\n\n**Assessment of the construction itself:** The idea is plausible, but the verification that every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of these lines is flawed (see Cases\u202f2 and\u202f3 below). Moreover, the construction fails for certain admissible values of \\(k\\) (e.g. \\(k=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) when \\(n\\) is even).\n\n### 7. Verification \u2013 Case\u202f1 (\\(0\\le d\\le k-1\\))\nCorrect: If \\(d=b-a\\) lies in \\([0,k-1]\\), the point satisfies \\(y = x + d\\), i.e. lies on sunny line \\(S_d\\). No issue.\n\n### 8. Verification \u2013 Case\u202f2 (\\(d\\ge k\\))\nThe solution writes:\n\\[\na\\le\\frac{n+1-d}{2}\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le n-2k .\n\\]\n\n* The first inequality follows from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b=a+d\\).  \n* The second inequality uses \\(d\\ge k\\).  \n* The **third inequality** \\(\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le n-2k\\) is claimed to follow from \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).\n\n**Counterexample:** Take \\(n=6,\\;k=3\\) (allowed by the claimed answer). Then \\(\\frac{n+1-k}{2}=\\frac{7-3}{2}=2\\) while \\(n-2k=0\\); the inequality \\(2\\le0\\) is false. Hence the chain breaks, and we cannot guarantee that \\(a\\) lies in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-2k\\}\\). Consequently the point need not lie on any vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\nIndeed, for \\(n=6,\\;k=3\\) the construction provides **no vertical lines** (since \\(n-2k=0\\)). The point \\((1,6)\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_6\\) (because \\(1+6=7\\le7\\)) and has \\(d=5\\ge k\\). It is not on any sunny line (differences covered are \\(0,1,2\\)) and there are no horizontal lines with \\(y=6\\). Hence \\((1,6)\\) is uncovered.  \n\n**Classification:** Critical Error \u2013 the inequality is unjustified and the case analysis does not hold for all admissible \\(k\\).\n\n### 9. Verification \u2013 Case\u202f3 (\\(d\\le -1\\))\nThe solution argues:\n* Write \\(d=-\\delta\\) with \\(\\delta\\ge1\\).  \n* Uses the chain\n\\[\nb = a-\\delta \\le \\frac{n+1-\\delta}{2}\\le\\frac{n+1-(k+1)}{2}= \\frac{n-2k}{2}\\le k .\n\\]\n\nThe justification \u201c\\(\\delta\\ge k+1\\) which follows from \\(b<a\\) together with the fact that all differences \\(-1,-2,\\dots ,-(k)\\) are already covered by the sunny lines\u201d is **incorrect**:\n\n* Sunny lines \\(y=x+c\\) cover only **non\u2011negative** differences \\(c\\). They do **not** cover any negative difference.  \n* Therefore we cannot deduce \\(\\delta\\ge k+1\\) from the existence of sunny lines