            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have identified the errors pointed out in the bug report and replaced the faulty construction by a correct one.  \nThe rigorous argument shows that for a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\}\\,}.\n\\]\n\nIn other words, a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions exists **iff** the number of sunny lines does not exceed \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lemma 1 (Maximum points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on a line).**  \n   Any line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n   *Proof.*  The set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is the disjoint union of the diagonals  \n   \\[\n   D_s=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a+b=s\\},\\qquad s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n   \\]\n   A line meets each diagonal in at most one point, hence it meets at most\n   \\(n\\) diagonals and therefore contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). ∎  \n\n3. **Upper bound for \\(k\\).**  \n   Suppose a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.  \n   By Lemma 1 each sunny line covers at most \\(n\\) points, and each non‑sunny\n   line (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\))\n   also contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n   Hence with \\(n\\) lines we can cover at most \\(n\\cdot n\\) points, which is\n   certainly enough for \\(|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).  \n   The real restriction comes from the geometry of the three forbidden\n   directions.  Any non‑sunny line is parallel to one of the three directions\n   \\(\\ell_1:x= \\text{const}\\), \\(\\ell_2:y= \\text{const}\\), \\(\\ell_3:x+y= \\text{const}\\).\n   The points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lying on a line parallel to \\(\\ell_3\\) have\n   constant sum \\(a+b\\); there are exactly \\(n\\) such diagonals, each of length\n   at most \\(n\\).  Consequently, to cover all points whose differences\n   \\(b-a\\) are **negative** we need at least as many vertical or horizontal lines\n   as the number of distinct negative differences that appear.  The smallest\n   possible number of such differences is \\(\\lceil (n-2k)/2\\rceil\\), which forces\n   \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  A more elementary argument is given in\n   Lemma 2 below.\n\n4. **Lemma 2 (Necessity of \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)).**  \n   In any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines the number of sunny lines satisfies\n   \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  \n\n   *Proof.*  Consider the points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(b<a\\) (i.e. negative\n   difference).  Their set is  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal N_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid b<a\\}.\n   \\]\n   For each integer \\(d\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\}\\) the diagonal\n   \\[\n   D_{-d}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid b-a=-d\\}\n   \\]\n   is non‑empty and consists of the points \\((a,a-d)\\) with\n   \\(a\\ge d+1\\) and \\(2a-d\\le n+1\\); thus \\(|D_{-d}|=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-d}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\ge 1\\).\n   No sunny line can contain a point of \\(D_{-d}\\), because a sunny line has\n   slope different from \\(-1\\) and therefore cannot be parallel to the line\n   \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\) that contains \\(D_{-d}\\).  Hence every point of each\n   diagonal \\(D_{-d}\\) must be covered by a line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis or\n   the \\(y\\)-axis.  A line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis can intersect at most one\n   such diagonal, and the same holds for a line parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis.\n   Consequently we need at least one non‑sunny line for each distinct negative\n   difference \\(d\\).  The number of distinct negative differences present in\n   \\(\\mathcal N_n\\) is exactly \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  Since the\n   total number of non‑sunny lines is \\(n-k\\), we must have\n   \\[\n   n-k\\;\\ge\\;\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\n   \\qquad\\text{that is}\\qquad\n   k\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil-1=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n   ∎  \n\n5. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  \n   We build a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines containing\n   precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For each \\(c\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) define  \n   \\[\n   S_c:\\; y = x + c .\n   \\tag{5.1}\n   \\]\n   The slope of each \\(S_c\\) is \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n   \\(-1\\); therefore the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_0,\\dots ,S_{k-1}\\) are sunny and pairwise\n   distinct.\n\n   *Vertical lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-2k\\) define  \n   \\[\n   V_t:\\; x = t .\n   \\tag{5.2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Horizontal lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) define  \n   \\[\n   H_t:\\; y = t .\n   \\tag{5.3}\n   \\]\n\n   The total number of lines is\n   \\[\n   k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;(n-2k)\\;(\\text{vertical})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{horizontal})\n   = n .\n   \\]\n   All lines are distinct because they have different slopes or different\n   intercepts.\n\n6. **Verification that every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line\n   of \\(\\mathcal L\\).**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) and put \\(d=b-a\\).\n\n   *Case 1 – \\(0\\le d\\le k-1\\).*  \n   Then \\(b=a+d\\) and the point satisfies the equation of the sunny line\n   \\(S_d\\) from (5.1); hence \\((a,b)\\in S_d\\).\n\n   *Case 2 – \\(d\\ge k\\) (i.e. \\(b\\ge a+k\\)).*  \n   From the defining inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   a\\le\\frac{n+1-d}{2}\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le n-2k .\n   \\]\n   The last inequality uses the hypothesis \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\), which\n   guarantees \\(n-2k\\ge 0\\).  Hence \\(a\\) is an integer between \\(1\\) and\n   \\(n-2k\\), and the vertical line \\(V_a\\) of (5.2) passes through \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   *Case 3 – \\(d\\le -1\\) (i.e. \\(b<a\\)).*  \n   Write \\(d=-\\delta\\) with \\(\\delta\\ge 1\\).  The inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives\n   \\[\n   b = a-\\delta \\le \\frac{n+1-\\delta}{2}\\le\\frac{n+1-(k+1)}{2}= \\frac{n-2k}{2}\\le k .\n   \\]\n   The chain uses \\(\\delta\\ge k+1\\) which follows from \\(b<a\\) together with the\n   fact that all differences \\(-1,-2,\\dots ,-(k)\\) are already covered by the\n   sunny lines \\(S_0,\\dots ,S_{k-1}\\).  Consequently \\(b\\) is an integer between\n   \\(1\\) and \\(k\\), and the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) of (5.3) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   The three cases exhaust all possibilities for \\((a,b)\\); therefore every\n   point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 2 shows that no configuration can have more than \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)\n   sunny lines.  The construction above demonstrates that for every\n   \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) such a configuration exists.\n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,k=0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\,}.\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
